      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To study the association of human serum Lp(a) level and coronary heart diseases."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Objects examined were conposed of 2 groups: CHD (39 cases) and healthy controls (52 cases). Lp (a), TC, HDL-C, TG, apoB of two groups were determined and the results were done with statistic analysis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The mean serum Lp(a) concentrations (mg.L-1) in coronary heart disease group were shown higher significantly than that in the control group (P < 0.01). However, there were no significant correlation between the mean serum Lp(a) and the mean serum TC, HDL-C, TG, spoAI and aopB.(P > 0.05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Serum Lp(a) level is closely related to the occurrence of coronary heart disease. Lp(a) is a single risk factor for coronary heart disease. Detecting the determination of serum Lp(a) is extremely valuable to the clinical prediction and diagnosis of CHD."
        }
      ]},
